# Eastern Keel | Visual Design Language | 2026

*Standards verified June 2026. Assumes Tailwind CSS v4 and Next.js 16.2 App Router. Read this before any UI, component, page, or styling work.*

## Before you begin

Read the codebase first. If a brand palette already exists in an `@theme` block, in CSS variables, or in a legacy `tailwind.config`, use it. Do not invent colors. If nothing is defined, ask:

```
What are your brand colors and typefaces?
1. Primary color (hex or oklch)
2. Accent color (hex or oklch)
3. Background (default: warm off white)
4. Text (default: near black, never pure #000000)
5. Display typeface and body typeface, if chosen

Defaults if undefined:
- Background: #F5F4F0 (warm off white)
- Text: #1A1A1A (near black)
- Accent: supplied by you
- Display type: a variable grotesque or a high contrast variable serif
- Body type: the same family at a text optical size, or a neutral companion
```

---

## Design philosophy

Confident, quiet, editorial. Every layout decision is structural. Nothing is decorative. The page reads like a magazine spread, not a website template. Photography carries the visual weight. Typography is the architecture. White space is not empty. It is the design.

The reference aesthetic is high end fashion editorial meeting Swiss graphic design. Bold display type as a graphic element. Photography first. Thin rule lines as structure. Rotated sidebar text. Arrows as functional navigation markers, never decoration.

One shift for 2026: type is allowed to behave, not only sit. Variable fonts let a headline carry hierarchy through weight and optical size from a single file, and let one expressive display moment per page shift its weight as the reader arrives. This stays inside the rule that nothing is decorative, because the motion is structural and earns its place. It is covered in the animation document, referenced here so the type choices anticipate it.

---

## Typography

Typography is the largest change in this revision. The old approach loaded several static weight files. The 2026 approach is one variable font, with hierarchy expressed through its axes.

### Variable fonts are the infrastructure

Load one variable font per family. A variable font ships every weight and, where the face supports it, optical size and width, inside a single file. This is lighter than loading four static weights, and it lets headings and body share one face while reading correctly at every size, because the optical size axis thickens hairlines and opens spacing at small sizes and refines them at display sizes.

- Display and headings: a variable grotesque (Inter, GT America, Bricolage Grotesque) or, for a sharper editorial voice, a high contrast variable serif. The expressive serif revival suits the magazine aesthetic and reads as intentional rather than default.
- Body: the same family at its text optical size, or a single neutral companion. Never exceed two families.
- Load through `next/font`. It self hosts the file, so there is no external font request, applies `font-display: swap`, and generates a metric matched fallback so the swap causes zero layout shift.

```ts
// app/fonts.ts
import localFont from 'next/font/local'

export const display = localFont({
  src: './fonts/BrandVariable.woff2', // a single variable file
  variable: '--font-display',
  display: 'swap',
  weight: '100 900',           // the full weight axis range
})
```

Drive weight through the axis, not separate files:

```css
.h1 { font-weight: 680; }      /* any value in the axis range, not only 400/700 */
.lead { font-weight: 360; }
```

If the face exposes optical size, set it explicitly at the extremes rather than trusting `auto`:

```css
.display { font-variation-settings: "opsz" 144; }  /* display cut */
.body    { font-variation-settings: "opsz" 16; }   /* text cut */
```

### Line wrapping is now the platform's job

Two properties replace manual line breaking and are widely available in 2026. Use both. They are the cheapest quality upgrade in this entire system.

- `text-wrap: balance` on display type, headings, and short blockquotes. It evens ragged lines so a headline does not drop one orphan word onto its own line. Apply only to short text, since the browser caps how many lines it balances.
- `text-wrap: pretty` on body copy. It prevents single word last lines and short orphans across whole paragraphs without the cost of full balancing.

```css
h1, h2, h3, .display, blockquote { text-wrap: balance; }
p, li, .body { text-wrap: pretty; }
```

### Scale

- Display and hero: clamp(64px, 10vw, 160px). Architectural scale. Often bleeds off a screen edge or overlaps imagery.
- H1: clamp(40px, 6vw, 96px)
- H2: clamp(24px, 3.5vw, 48px)
- Body: 16px to 17px, line height 1.65 to 1.75
- Caption, label, meta: 11px to 12px, tracking 0.08em to 0.12em, uppercase or small caps
- Sidebar rotated label: 11px to 12px, letter spacing 0.1em, uppercase, rotated 90deg or 270deg

Fluid sizes use the theme text tokens. Root relative units (`rch`, `rcap`, `rex`, `ric`) are now available if you want sizing that tracks the root font, but `clamp` with viewport units remains the house default for fluid display type.

### Behavior

- Display type may overlap photography. Intentional.
- Display type may bleed off the right or bottom edge. Intentional.
- Never center body text.
- Headlines are left aligned or intentionally asymmetric.
- No text shadows over photography. Darken the image with an opacity overlay instead.
- No hyphens in copy, ever. Do not enable `hyphens: auto`.
- Avoid justified text. Ragged right with `text-wrap: pretty` reads better.

