## Color

- Use brand colors as provided or read from the codebase.
- Neutral first. Backgrounds are off white, warm white, or true white. Never gray blue, never cold.
- Accent is sparing. At most 15 to 25 percent of any layout.
- Color blocks are structural. A full bleed accent panel replaces a section. It is not a card background.
- Near black sections with reversed white type are the contrast moments: footer, feature callout, stat block.
- No gradients, anywhere.
- No drop shadows. One exception, a very subtle shadow on a floating UI element only: `0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.06)`.
- Thin rule lines (`#E0DED8` or 10 percent black) divide structure. Never as a box around a card.

### House color format and derivation

Define brand colors in `oklch`. It is what Tailwind v4 uses internally, and it interpolates cleanly, so a tint reads as the same hue rather than drifting muddy. Hex values from a brand sheet can be pasted as is.

Derive tints and shades with `color-mix` instead of hardcoding a second swatch. One brand token then generates its own hover and muted states, and a brand change updates everything downstream:

```css
.btn-solid:hover { background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-accent), black 10%); }
.surface-muted  { background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-accent), white 88%); }
```

### Theming with light-dark

If the project supports a dark mode, prefer the `light-dark()` function over duplicated class trees. Declare `color-scheme` once and let each token resolve:

```css
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }

@theme {
  --color-background: light-dark(#F5F4F0, #0A0A0A);
  --color-text:       light-dark(#1A1A1A, #F2F1EC);
  --color-rule:       light-dark(#E0DED8, rgba(255,255,255,0.14));
}
```

This is cleaner than a `dark:` variant on every element and keeps the contrast moments coherent across modes. Reserve `dark:` variants for the few places a token cannot express the change.

