## Component patterns

### Navigation

Horizontal, full width, 1px bottom rule. Logo left, links right, or centered with the logo above on editorial layouts. Links at 12px to 13px, uppercase, tracking 0.08em. No background fill in the default state. Transparent over the hero, white on scroll with the 1px rule appearing on scroll. Mobile opens a full screen overlay, not a sidebar drawer.

### Hero

Always full viewport height (`min-h-dvh`). Photography fills the frame, no box, no container. The headline sits on the image or splits 60/40 beside it. One CTA: a text link with an arrow, or a ghost button.

### Buttons

Two styles only.

1. **Ghost:** 1px border, no fill, text and an optional arrow. Hover fills with the accent or near black and inverts the text.
2. **Solid:** accent or near black fill, white text, radius 0 to 2px. Hover darkens 10 percent via `color-mix`.

No pill buttons. No radius beyond 2px. No gradient fill. No shadow. Minimum height 44px for touch.

### Forms and inputs

- 1px bottom border only. Underline style, no full box border, radius 0.
- Label above the input at 11px uppercase, tracking 0.08em.
- Focus transitions the border to the accent or near black.
- Error state uses a brand palette color, never the default browser red unless the brand defines it.
- Input font size 16px minimum. This prevents the iOS zoom on focus.
- `field-sizing: content` on text inputs and textareas, so a field grows to fit what is typed instead of scrolling inside a fixed box. It keeps the underline aesthetic intact while removing the cramped single line textarea.

```css
input[type="text"], input[type="email"], textarea {
  field-sizing: content;
  min-height: 2.75rem;
}
```

### Selects, menus, tooltips, popovers

Two platform features now remove the JavaScript that used to position and manage these. Reach for them before any library.

**Customizable select.** With `appearance: base-select`, a native `<select>` can be styled to match the underline aesthetic and can hold richer option content, while keeping native keyboard navigation, focus return, and the picker that the platform positions for you. Use it for editorial dropdowns rather than a scripted custom select.

**Anchor positioning** tethers a popover, menu, or tooltip to its trigger in pure CSS, with an automatic fallback position so it never overflows the viewport. Pair it with the Popover API, which handles open, close, light dismiss, and focus, so a menu needs no positioning JavaScript at all:

```css
.menu-trigger { anchor-name: --menu; }

.menu {
  position: absolute;
  position-anchor: --menu;
  top: anchor(bottom);
  justify-self: anchor-center;
  position-try-fallbacks: flip-block, flip-inline;  /* stay on screen */
}
```

```html
<button popovertarget="menu" class="menu-trigger">Menu</button>
<nav id="menu" popover class="menu"> ... </nav>
```

The entrance and exit animation for these lives in the animation document, using `@starting-style`. Anchor positioning places them. The Popover API opens them. `@starting-style` animates them. None of it needs a positioning library.

### Cards

Use sparingly. Prefer open layouts. No radius beyond 4px. No shadow. No background fill in light sections, let the image be the card. If a background is needed, a 1px `#E0DED8` border. The image is full bleed at the top with no radius.

### Images

- `next/image` always, with real `alt` text.
- No radius on editorial images, ever. No shadow.
- Aspect ratios: 3:4 portrait, 16:9 landscape, 1:1 grid, 4:5 product. `object-fit: cover` always.
- Hover on a linked image: scale 1.02, 400ms, image only, never the container.
- The LCP or hero image uses `loading="eager"` and `fetchPriority="high"`. Next.js 16 deprecated the `priority` prop in favor of `preload`, so prefer the eager and fetchPriority pair.
- AVIF first with WebP fallback, configured site wide in `next.config`.

